WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.711
Book Three, Proposition Twenty-Three.

2
00:00:02.711 --> 00:00:10.203
On the same segment, and on the same side of it, there cannot be constructed two similar and unequal segments of circles.

3
00:00:10.203 --> 00:00:18.810
For, if possible, on the same segment A B and on the same side, let two similar and unequal circle segments A C B and A D B be constructed.

4
00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:27.720
Draw the line A C D cutting through both arcs at C and D, and join the segments C B and D B by Postulate One.

5
00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:43.669
Because circle segment A C B is similar to circle segment A D B, and by Book Three, Definition Eleven similar segments of circles are those which admit equal angles, the inscribed angle A C B equals the inscribed angle A D B.

6
00:00:43.669 --> 00:00:57.128
Yet in triangle C D B, angle A C B is an exterior angle and angle A D B is its opposite interior angle; so an exterior angle equals an opposite interior angle, which is impossible by One Sixteen.

7
00:00:57.128 --> 00:01:06.860
Therefore, on the same segment and on the same side, two similar and unequal circle segments cannot be constructed, as was required to prove.

8
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:20.041
Proposition Twenty-Three is the circular counterpart of Book One, Proposition Seven: just as side lengths uniquely rigidify a triangle on a base, the inscribed angle uniquely determines the circle segment on a chord.

9
00:01:20.041 --> 00:01:34.692
As Robert Simson noted in Heath's commentary, because the two circles already intersect at A and B, Three Ten guarantees they cannot cross again—forcing one arc wholly inside the other and setting up the exterior-angle contradiction of One Sixteen.

10
00:01:34.692 --> 00:01:45.680
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred twenty-eight synthesizes forty ancestor nodes, bridging Definition Eleven's similar circle segments with Book One's exterior angle inequality.

